1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,444
A surveyor on the bank of the Narayani wants the distance to a tree on the far side.

2
00:00:05,444 --> 00:00:07,983
No boat, no rope across the water.

3
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:16,769
Instead she walks fifty metres along her bank and measures two angles: seventy degrees at one end, sixty at the other.

4
00:00:16,769 --> 00:00:21,570
One side and two angles: the triangle is now completely decided.

5
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:31,111
The third angle is fifty degrees, and the sine law turns those angles into the missing sides: about fifty six and a half metres.

6
00:00:31,111 --> 00:00:36,047
This unit is the dictionary that translates between sides and angles.

7
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:46,573
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 6: Properties of a Triangle.

8
00:00:48,967 --> 00:00:50,049
Six parts.

9
00:00:50,049 --> 00:01:02,263
The sine law, the cosine law, the tangent law, the projection laws, the half angle laws, and finally the area of a triangle with its circumcircle, incircle and excircles.

10
00:01:02,263 --> 00:01:07,473
One triangle, thirteen, fourteen, fifteen, will run through all of them.

11
00:01:11,833 --> 00:01:13,280
Standard notation.

12
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:18,215
Side a faces angle A, b faces B, c faces C.

13
00:01:18,215 --> 00:01:25,142
The angles add to one eighty, s is the semi perimeter, and delta is the area.

14
00:01:25,142 --> 00:01:31,693
Our running triangle has sides thirteen, fourteen and fifteen: its numbers come out unusually clean.

15
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:34,805
Drop the altitude from A, length h.

16
00:01:34,805 --> 00:01:39,284
In the left right-angled triangle, h is c sin B.

17
00:01:39,284 --> 00:01:42,136
In the right one, h is b sin C.

18
00:01:42,136 --> 00:01:46,954
They are the same length, so b over sin B equals c over sin C.

19
00:01:46,954 --> 00:01:52,658
Do the same from another vertex and all three ratios are equal: the sine law.

20
00:01:53,867 --> 00:01:55,874
Why are the three ratios equal?

21
00:01:55,874 --> 00:02:06,330
Draw the circle through A, B and C, the circumcircle, with radius R. Keep B and C fixed and slide A round the circle.

22
00:02:06,330 --> 00:02:11,369
The angle at A does not change, and neither does a over sin A.

23
00:02:11,369 --> 00:02:14,026
It always equals the diameter, two R.

24
00:02:14,233 --> 00:02:17,998
The proof: take A prime at the end of the diameter through B.

25
00:02:17,998 --> 00:02:26,279
The angle at A prime equals the angle at A, standing on the same chord, and the angle at C is a right angle, in a semicircle.

26
00:02:26,279 --> 00:02:27,987
So sin A is a over two R.

27
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:40,381
So a over sin A, b over sin B and c over sin C all equal two R. Written as a equals two R sin A, it is the switch that turns any side into a sine.

28
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:45,055
Two angles and a side: the sine law's home ground.

29
00:02:45,267 --> 00:02:48,149
First the third angle: a hundred and five degrees.

30
00:02:48,367 --> 00:02:55,499
Then b over sin B equals a over sin A: ten root two, fourteen point one four.

31
00:02:55,700 --> 00:03:02,902
And c, using sin one oh five, point nine six five nine: nineteen point three two.

32
00:03:02,902 --> 00:03:06,222
The largest side faces the largest angle.

33
00:03:06,222 --> 00:03:06,607
Check.

34
00:03:08,633 --> 00:03:09,637
An identity.

35
00:03:09,637 --> 00:03:11,981
Turn every side into a sine.

36
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:16,300
Use a equals two R sin A, and the same for b and c.

37
00:03:16,533 --> 00:03:17,889
Expand the bracket.

38
00:03:17,889 --> 00:03:21,674
Every product appears twice, with opposite signs.

39
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:22,873
So it is zero.

40
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:35,186
The sine law cannot help when you know all three sides, or two sides and the angle between them.

41
00:03:35,186 --> 00:03:37,308
For that we need the cosine law.

42
00:03:37,308 --> 00:03:40,407
Drop the altitude from C onto A B.

43
00:03:40,407 --> 00:03:50,932
The blue piece A D is b cos A, the altitude is b sin A, and the rest of the base, D B, is c minus b cos A.

44
00:03:51,133 --> 00:03:59,598
Pythagoras in the right triangle on the right: a squared equals b sin A squared, plus c minus b cos A squared.

45
00:03:59,598 --> 00:04:00,601
Expand.

46
00:04:00,601 --> 00:04:09,068
The sin squared and cos squared combine into one, and a squared equals b squared plus c squared, minus two b c cos A.

47
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:12,140
Think of it as Pythagoras with a correction.

48
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:18,325
At ninety degrees cos A is zero, and it is exactly Pythagoras.

49
00:04:18,325 --> 00:04:23,351
Make A acute and a squared is less than b squared plus c squared.

50
00:04:23,351 --> 00:04:31,033
Make A obtuse: cos A turns negative, the foot D slides outside the base, and a squared becomes bigger.

51
00:04:31,033 --> 00:04:33,577
The same formula covers every case.

52
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:39,697
Rearranged, the cosine law gives any angle from the three sides.

53
00:04:39,697 --> 00:04:43,961
Notice the pattern: the side opposite the angle is the one subtracted.

54
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:46,109
The running triangle.

55
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:50,855
Cos A is two fifty two over four twenty: three fifths.

56
00:04:50,855 --> 00:04:52,972
A is fifty three degrees eight minutes.

57
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:55,062
Cos C is five thirteenths.

58
00:04:55,267 --> 00:04:59,442
Sin C is positive in a triangle, so it is twelve thirteenths.

59
00:05:01,467 --> 00:05:03,659
Two sides and the angle between them.

60
00:05:03,867 --> 00:05:09,780
The cosine law: forty nine plus sixty four minus fifty six: fifty seven.

61
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,220
a is root fifty seven, seven point five five centimetres.

62
00:05:16,233 --> 00:05:17,328
Keep the sign.

63
00:05:17,328 --> 00:05:24,287
A negative cosine means an obtuse angle: minus a half is one hundred and twenty degrees, not sixty.

64
00:05:30,067 --> 00:05:36,345
Given two sides and the angle between them, the tangent law finds both other angles at once.

65
00:05:36,345 --> 00:05:45,372
Start from the sine law: a minus b over a plus b is sin A minus sin B over sin A plus sin B; the two R cancels.

66
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:49,208
Turn the sum and difference of sines into products.

67
00:05:49,208 --> 00:05:55,608
The twos cancel, leaving cot of half A plus B, times tan of half A minus B.

68
00:05:55,833 --> 00:06:01,600
Half of A plus B is ninety minus half C, so that cotangent is tan of half C.

69
00:06:01,833 --> 00:06:08,460
Therefore tan of half A minus B equals a minus b over a plus b, times cot of half C.

70
00:06:09,667 --> 00:06:10,868
The tangent law at work.

71
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:15,582
Half the sum of A and B is ninety minus thirty: sixty degrees.

72
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:23,461
Half the difference: two sixteenths times root three, point two one six five, so twelve degrees thirteen minutes.

73
00:06:23,667 --> 00:06:29,725
Add and subtract: A is seventy two thirteen, B is forty seven forty seven.

74
00:06:29,933 --> 00:06:34,723
And c from the cosine law: root sixty seven, eight point one nine.

